## Why This Is Asked
Tests a practical KCNA routing pattern: per-message fan-out with per-destination ordering and safe offset commits.

## Key Concepts
- Per-destination routing and ordering
- Offset management: commit after downstream ack
- Outbox pattern and idempotent sinks
- Retries and backoff
- Canary testing for correctness

## Code Example
```javascript
class RouteFanoutProcessor {
  onMessage(msg) {
    const dest = msg.headers.dest;
    const target = `sink-${dest}`;
    return publish(target, msg.payload).then(() => ack(msg.offset));
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you scale to many destinations and hot keys?
- How would you guarantee approximate exactly-once with external sinks?
- How would you instrument latency and ordering per destination?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical producer-side rate limiting, per-topic isolation, and basic backpressure, all at a beginner level but with real constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Per-topic state and ordering guarantees
- Token bucket rate limiting and refill logic
- Backpressure strategies: accept/queue vs reject with retry
- Minimal test plan to validate burst handling and latency

## Code Example
```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(capacity, rate) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.rate = rate; // tokens per second
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryRemove(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this to fairly share tokens among many topics?
- How would you observe and alert on burst spikes and depletion across topics?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests windowed joins, multi-partition state, watermarking, backpressure, and offset semantics in KCNA; relevant to large-scale streaming platforms.

## Key Concepts
- Windowed join with event-time
- Watermarks and lateness tolerance
- Per-user state store
- Offset preservation and at-least-once semantics
- Partitioning and rebalances
- Canary testing for late and out-of-order data

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo KCNA sink skeleton
class EnrichmentSink {
  constructor() { this.profileStore = new Map(); }
  onMessage(clickEvent) {
    const p = this.profileStore.get(clickEvent.userId);
    if (p) {
      this.emit({ ...clickEvent, profile: p });
    } else {
      // could hold or emit with null profile depending on policy
    }
  }
  onProfileUpdate(userId, profile) {
    this.profileStore.set(userId, profile);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle missing profile data for a click and for how long would you wait?
- How would you scale window size and watermark strategy for 1000 partitions while preserving latency goals?

## Why This Is Asked

Assess the candidate's ability to design per-tenant fairness in a high-throughput KCNA pipeline, balancing isolation, latency, and dynamic scaling under bursty traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant token-bucket quotas and fair queuing
- Backpressure signaling (429s, retry-after) and quota persistence
- Telemetry-driven dynamic rebalancing and burst forgiveness
- Canary-based validation and observability

## Code Example

```javascript
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryConsume(n = 1) {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test dynamic quota rebalancing under tenant churn?
- How do you prevent misbehaving tenants from starving others while preserving low latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of schema versioning, dead-letter routing, and offset discipline in KCNA.

## Key Concepts

- Versioned in-memory JSON schema registry
- Payload validation with a JSON schema validator
- Dead-letter routing to invalid_events
- Offset management and at-least-once semantics
- Lightweight deduplication for idempotence

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton illustrating onMessage flow
function onMessage(msg) {
  const payload = JSON.parse(msg.value);
  const version = payload.schemaVersion;
  const schema = registry.get(msg.topic + '|' + version);
  if (!schema) { publishInvalid(msg, 'Unknown schemaVersion'); return; }
  if (!validate(payload, schema)) { publishInvalid(msg, 'Validation failed'); return; }
  publishMain(msg);
  commitOffsets(msg);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evolve the registry for many topics? 
- How would you test canary scenarios, including missing schemas and duplicate messages?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design robust multi-tenant data quality controls, handles schema drift, and preserves latency SLAs under backpressure.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant schema catalogs and versioning
- QualityGate operator contracts and DLQ routing
- Backward-compatible schema evolution and drift metrics
- Latency budgeting and backpressure handling
- Canary testing with synthetic tenants

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: QualityGate interface for KCNA
class QualityGate {
  constructor(schemaCatalog){ this.catalog = schemaCatalog }
  validate(event){ const schema = this.catalog.get(event.tenantId, event.version); return schema ? schema.validate(event) : false }
  route(event, valid){ return valid ? event : { error: 'schema-violation' } }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure drift latency and false positives in production?
- How do you handle a tenant upgrading to a new schema mid-stream without dropping data?

## Why This Is Asked
Regulated multi-tenant data requires per-tenant privacy controls with verifiable audit trails and deterministic replay.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped redaction
- Deterministic replay via sequence numbers
- Cryptographic receipts and audit roots
- Per-tenant policy evaluation
- Latency implications and backpressure handling

## Code Example
```javascript
function redact(record, policy){
  const out = {...record};
  if(policy.redactEmail) out.email = 'REDACTED';
  if(policy.redactIp) out.ip = 'REDACTED';
  if(policy.redactPhone) out.phone = 'REDACTED';
  // tokenization could be added here
  return out;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to version policies without downtime?
- How to quantify privacy leakage vs. latency trade-offs?
- How would you test audit integrity under partial failures?

## Why This Is Asked

This explores privacy-preserving cross-tenant analytics, auditing, and key management in KCNA at scale, aligning with enterprise needs around data sharing without leakage.

## Key Concepts

- Differential privacy and secure aggregation
- Per-tenant access controls and key rotation
- Tamper-evident auditing of query results
- API design for aggregated analytics
- Performance considerations under multi-tenant load

## Code Example

```javascript
// Demonstrative DP aggregation stub
function clipAndNoisify(values, epsilon, sensitivity) {
  const clipped = values.map(v => Math.max(-sensitivity, Math.min(sensitivity, v)));
  const b = sensitivity / epsilon;
  const noise = (Math.random() < 0.5 ? -1 : 1) * b * Math.log(1 - Math.random());
  return clipped.reduce((a,b)=>a+b,0) + noise;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure privacy budget consumption over time in a live dashboard?
- What changes would you make to support streaming DP with backpressure and multi-tenant isolation?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests stateful per-key windowing, dynamic rule handling, and exactly-once guarantees under late messages.

## Key Concepts
- Per-account sliding window sums
- Dynamic rule lookup and on-the-fly threshold changes
- Watermarks for late arrivals and eviction
- Exactly-once semantics and idempotent fraud_alerts
- Backpressure and test canary design

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo: per-account rolling sum with 5m sliding window
state = Map(); // accountId -> [{ts, amt}]
rules = Map(); // accountId -> threshold
onEvent(tx) {
  cleanExpired(tx.ts);
  sum = sumAmt(state.get(tx.accountId));
  threshold = rules.get(tx.accountId) ?? default;
  if (sum > threshold) emitFraud(tx.accountId, sum, threshold);
  append(state, tx.accountId, {ts: tx.ts, amt: tx.amount});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle simultaneous rule updates and transactions to avoid inconsistent alerts?
- How would you scale to millions of accounts and high-throughput streams while maintaining low latency?

